    "https://easternctstateuniversity.applytojob.com/apply/c2hDqlYEU5/Assistant-Professor-Of-Energy-And-Environmental-Science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science - Eastern CT State University - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Eastern CT State University Thanks for visiting our Career Page. Please review our open positions and apply to the positions that match your qualifications. Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science Willimantic, CT Full Time Experienced Share Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science Eastern Connecticut State University is seeking a qualified candidate to fill the Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science position. Eastern is Connecticut’s Public Liberal Arts University and is located in Willimantic, Connecticut which is 1.5 hours from Boston and 2.5 hours from New York City.  Eastern is one of four universities in the Connecticut State College and University System (CSCU). The position also offers substantial health and retirement benefits. Tenure-Track, Full-Time Position The Department of Environmental Earth Science (EES) at Eastern Connecticut State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science, beginning August 2027. We seek a teacher-scholar whose academic focus concerns earth systems, energy resources, and sustainability.  The successful candidate will help shape an innovative undergraduate curriculum while mentoring students through original research, experiential learning, and community-engagement. Responsibilities Teach four undergraduate courses per semester in sustainable energy, energy resources, climate, and environmental earth science Contribute to Eastern’s liberal arts curriculum Develop new courses aligned with expertise Maintain active scholarship including with undergraduate students Mentor undergraduate research and internships Contribute to the Sustainable Energy Studies Minor and university service Collaborate with the Center for Sustainability Studies Areas of Potential Research Renewable and sustainable energy resources and systems Critical minerals Climate-energy interactions Environmental impacts of energy development Qualifications Ph.D. in a relevant field by appointment date Potential for excellence in undergraduate teaching Active research agenda Willingness to embrace Eastern’s liberal arts mission About the Department For more than five decades, Eastern’s EES Department has built a reputation for outstanding undergraduate education grounded in field experiences, close faculty-student mentoring, and interdisciplinary inquiry. With approximately 900 alumni and one of the largest undergraduate geoscience programs in New England, the department prepares graduates for careers in environmental consulting, geoscience, sustainability, education, GIS, government, and industry. Faculty integrate geology, hydrology, climatology, oceanography, GIS, environmental chemistry, ecology, and policy to examine Earth as an interconnected system. Students routinely participate in faculty research, field studies, and extended field courses. The department is home to the Center for Sustainability Studies and provides leadership to the Green Campus Committee. Why Eastern As Connecticut’s public liberal arts university, Eastern emphasizes close interaction between faculty and students, experiential learning, and interdisciplinary collaboration. The university’s location offers exceptional natural laboratories including bedrock geology, glacial landscapes, coastal environments, groundwater systems, and the rapidly evolving offshore wind and climate resilience initiatives of southern New England. Faculty enjoy the opportunity to build meaningful careers focused on teaching, scholarship, and student success. Application Submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching philosophy, research interests (including undergraduate involvement), and contact information for three referees to JazzHR at: Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science - Eastern CT State University - Career Page . Application review begins December 1, 2026, and continues until the position is filled. Eastern Connecticut State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering an inclusive academic community. Compensation and Employee Benefits The Assistant Professor of Energy and Environmental Science is compensated at the Assistant Professor salary level in accordance with Connecticut State University American Association of University Professors (CSU-AAUP) Collective Bargaining Unit Agreement. For more information, please visit Bargaining Agreements/Pay Plans - Eastern . The State of Connecticut and Eastern Connecticut State University are proud to offer its employees competitive wages and a wide range of fringe benefits including voluntary and supplemental benefits. Fringe benefits include: paid personal, vacation and sick leave accruals; medical, dental, and prescription plans; group life insurance; retirement; retiree health care; tuition waiver for employee and eligible dependents at any of the Connecticut State Universities (Central, Eastern, Southern or Western) as well as an array of voluntary and supplemental benefits such as Deferred Retirement Contribution plans, Dependent Care Assistance and Medical Flexible spending accounts; long and short term disability insurance plans. For more detailed information, please visit Human Resources - Eastern . Eastern Connecticut State University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religious creed, age, gender, gender identity or expression, national origin, marital status, ancestry, present or past history of mental disorder, learning disability or physical disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, genetic information or criminal record. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: LaMar Coleman, Ed.D., Vice President for Equity and Diversity. Dr. Coleman can be reached at: 860-465-5112 or via email at: [email protected] . Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file Cover Letter * What's your citizenship / employment eligibility? * No answer I am a U.S. Citizen/Permanent Resident Non-citizen allowed to work for any employer Non-citizen allowed to work for current employer Non-citizen seeking work authorization I am a Canadian Citizen/Permanent Resident Other References: Please enter names and contact information: * Will you have a Ph.D. in a relevant field by appointment date? * Yes No Do you have a active research agenda? * Yes No Do you have an understanding of a public liberal arts college mission along with the ability to contribute to the mission? * Yes No Are you legally authorized to work in the United States? * Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status? (e.g., H-1B visa status) Please note that CSCU does not provide sponsorship. * Yes No The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Lat